r1: stub -> recursive carries
	note=A example.com?
	message=sync
	description=One recursive query over UDP port 53. The stub waits for the full answer.
r2: recursive -> root carries
	note=A example.com?
	message=sync
	description=With nothing cached, the resolver starts at the root. It asks the full question; the root answers only the part it knows.
r3: root -> recursive produces
	note=refer: .com NS a.gtld-servers.net
	message=reply
	description=A referral, not an answer: the names and addresses of the .com servers.
r4: recursive -> tld carries
	note=A example.com?
	message=sync
r5: tld -> recursive produces
	note=refer: example.com NS ns1.example.com
	message=reply
	description=Another referral, one level down the name.
r6: recursive -> auth carries
	note=A example.com?
	message=sync
r7: auth -> recursive produces
	note=A 93.184.216.34  TTL 3600
	message=reply
	description=The authoritative answer. The resolver caches it, and every referral on the way, for its TTL.
r8: recursive -> auth carries
	note=same query over TCP
	message=sync
	fragment=opt:answer truncated
	description=Only when the UDP answer set the TC bit. This retry first needs a TCP handshake; open TCP fallback in the chain view to see it.
r9: recursive -> stub produces
	note=A 93.184.216.34
	message=reply
	description=The cold path cost three extra round trips inside the resolver. The browser only sees the total.
